<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Merseburg Charms</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th–10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> Charms are two magic spells in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a>, written down in the 9th or 10th century in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the cathedral library of Merseburg. One frees captives, the other heals a horse's injured foot by invoking the gods <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woden">Wodan</a>, Frija and others. Discovered in 1841, they are the only surviving pagan spells in Old High German.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merseburg-charms.jpg" alt="Merseburg Charms" width="320" height="507" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Merseburger_Zauberspr%C3%BCche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
